  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM EDT Fri Jun 19 2026\n\nUpstream shortwave trough over the northern Plains will move quickly \nsoutheast into the southern Great Lakes later tonight into Saturday, \nbringing increasing precipitation chances.  Precipitable water \nincreases toward 1.0\" late tonight with showers becoming more \nnumerous over portions of northern lower by late evening into the \novernight hours.  The deepest moisture and forcing slide along and \nsouth of M-72 tonight and this is where we will see the greatest \nshower coverage. The shortwave continues moving through the area on \nSaturday with a 925-850mb theta-e ridge axis extending from Saginaw \nBay northwest into the higher terrain of northern lower.  Steepening \nmid level lapse rates during the afternoon hours with daytime \nheating, combined with the deeper moisture and enhanced low level \nconvergence, will increase coverage of showers/storms across parts \nof northern lower. Although the best instability parameters and \nkinematics sit well south of our area, skinny CAPEs to 600-800 j/kg \nwill likely result in a few thunderstorms.  With relatively low \nfreezing levels around 7-8K, small hail would be possible with some \nof the stronger storms. Severe potential looks low overall. Any \nshower/storm activity will end quickly Saturday evening with the \nloss of daytime heating and drier air working into the area from the \nwest.  Patchy fog possible Saturday night.  \n\nNorthern Michigan gets \"squeezed\" between systems on Sunday with an \nupper low north of Superior and a stronger area of low pressure \nriding along a frontal boundary across the northern Ohio Valley. \nDrier air and subsidence across our area leading to partly to mostly \nsunny skies and mild temperatures.  We will likely see an increase \nin clouds over southern areas during the afternoon as that low \npressure system passes to our south.  The northern fringe of the \nprecipitation from that system to our south may skirt far southern \nparts of our area Sunday night with a shower threat (M-55 and points \nsouth), otherwise dry.  \n\nA fast and active northern jet stream will bring additional \nshortwaves into the Great Lakes this upcoming week as troughiness \nand below normal temperatures continue across northern Michigan. \nBest rain chances appear to be toward midweek but the timing and \nplacement of these shortwaves can be difficult in this type of flow.",
